.TH NYLEX 1 "October 2026" "nylex 0.2"
.SH NAME
nylex \- word processor in the style of LEX
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nylex
[
.B \-f
.I system.vmf
] [
.B \-u
.I terminal
] [
.B \-w
.I workfile
] [
.B \-c
.BR swe7 | latin1 | utf8
] [
.I document
]
.br
.B nylex
.BI /F system.vmf
.BI /U terminal
.BI /W workfile
.BI // keys //
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Nylex
is a word processor made in the image of LEX, the word processor of
Ace Microsystems Ltd (written in its Filetab-D language), which DIAD AB
sold in Sweden as LEX-68 for DIAB's D-NIX machines.
It was written from scratch for the D-NIX Amiga port after LEX's
technical manual (version 9, 1985); none of LEX's code is in it.
It reads and writes LEX's documents and its system files, LEX-68's own
.B lex9a1.vmf
among them, and works as the manual describes: a screen editor driven
by keystrokes, and programs of keystrokes (in keystores and in the
records of the system file) that make up menus, forms, list processing
and mailshot.
.PP
Without a
.I document
.I nylex
starts with the first menu (\c
.BR **MENU ).
A character picks a line of a menu; ESC E or Ctrl U goes back a menu,
and leaves the first one.
At a menu, / and a menu switch option (see below) can be typed too.
With a
.I document
.I nylex
goes straight to editing it.
.PP
Documents are text files in the current directory, named in lower case
(\c
.B BREV.BRV
is the file
.BR brev.brv ;
a name without a dot gets the default type,
.B .BRV
in nylex's own system file).
NAME/*.EXT uses the details of another document type.
A document starts with a ruler, a line of ruler characters across the
screen:
.RS
.nf
.if t .ft CR
.if t .ps -2
F....................................................................M
.if t .ps
.if t .ft R
.fi
.RE
F where paragraphs start, with a ragged right margin (J: justified),
M the right margin, L tab stops, R right-aligned tab stops, T tab stops
not used in insert mode, C centring (to M), H the hyphenation zone, and
.B ####,###.##
a numeric field (Tab into it starts the calculator).
A tab stop between F and M indents the first line of a paragraph.
Each ruler holds for the text under it.
Saving keeps the previous version as
.IR name .BAK
(the backup type of
.BR **INST ).
Attributes (underline, bold ...) are kept as LEX keeps them: the line
ends in the character 255 and the next line holds the attributes.
.PP
The system file (.VMF, 512-byte blocks of keyed records) holds the
menus, help pages, forms, printers, messages, rulers and abbreviations
of each document type, dictionaries and output formats for list
processing, data records, and the document index: for each document
its title and description, the dates it was made, changed, read and
printed and how many times, the time spent on it, and its lines, words
and characters.
By default it is
.BR $HOME/nylex.vmf ,
copied the first time from one that comes with
.IR nylex :
.B nylex.vmf
in Swedish when the locale is Swedish (\c
.B $LC_ALL,
.B $LC_MESSAGES
or
.B $LANG
starts with
.B sv
or names
.BR _SE ),
else
.B nylex-en.vmf
in English (on the D-NIX Amiga port, without a locale, Swedish).
A system file named with
.B \-f
that is not there yet is copied from the one of that name, if there is
one (\c
.BR "nylex \-f nylex-en.vmf" ).
The work file (\c
.BR $HOME/nylex.wrk )
keeps the keystores, rulers 6-9, the cut buffers, the search strings
and the calculator's memories from one session to the next.
.SH "MENU SWITCH OPTIONS"
Column 1 of a menu line says what the line does, columns 2-11 name a
record for it; typed at a menu as /x they ask for what they need.
.TP 8
C E I V
make, change, change (first line), view a document
.TP
K R A J
delete documents, rename, copy and edit, copy
.TP
P Q
print a document, several copies
.TP
X
the playground, running the record named (a program)
.TP
M U
another menu; another terminal record (\c
.BR )NAME )
.TP
S L
list processing with an output format; mailshot with a document
.TP
F Z G
another system file; another work file; leave for a program
.TP
1 2
another **MONT, **INST or **PNST; clear the work file
.TP
T W
statistics of the system file; the state of nylex
.TP
0 3 4
unlock records; reorganise the system file; copy records between
system files
.TP
6
convert a document with *CONV
.TP
B D H N
run a program (the record's first 32 characters) on documents
.SH "CONTROL KEYS"
.TP 18
Ctrl U J H R
up, down, left, right (the arrow keys too)
.TP
Ctrl F, Ctrl B
next word, word before
.TP
Ctrl G
end of the line
.TP
Ctrl L, Ctrl T
a screen forward, back (Page Down, Page Up)
.TP
Ctrl Q, Ctrl S
top, end of the document
.TP
Return, Tab
start of the next line; next tab stop (aligning text typed after C and
R stops, the calculator in # fields)
.TP
Ctrl A, Ctrl X
a blank line in, the line out
.TP
Ctrl D, Ctrl P
the character out (P: the paragraph closes up)
.TP
Ctrl E, Ctrl N
a blank in (N: the paragraph moves on)
.TP
Ctrl W, Ctrl V
the word, the rest of the line out
.TP
Rubout
the character to the left out
.TP
Ctrl Y
the character above copied
.TP
Ctrl K
the word changes case
.TP
Ctrl O, Ctrl Z
save and leave; leave without saving
.TP
Ctrl C
stop a program
.SH "ESCAPE SEQUENCES"
ESC is pressed and let go before the next key.
.TP 18
ESC E, ESC Z
save and leave; leave without saving
.TP
ESC V, ESC Q X
save and start again; the same, at the same line
.TP
ESC I, ESC - I
insert mode on, off
.TP
ESC J, ESC Q J
justify the paragraph; from the cursor
.TP
ESC Return, ESC Ctrl J
to the left margin of the next line; split the paragraph
.TP
ESC \e, ESC - \e
vertical typing on, off
.TP
ESC @, ESC #, ESC &
centre, right-align between tab stops; wide line
.TP
ESC M n, ESC W n, ESC U n
ruler n put in; the line stored as ruler n; ruler n used
.TP
ESC S G, ESC - S G
ignore the rulers in the document, use them again
.TP
ESC S ( n, ESC < n
mark position n; go back to it
.TP
ESC Q A, ESC Q B
copy the line above, below
.TP
ESC Q E, ESC Q M
end, top of the document
.TP
ESC Q F, ESC Q G
next paragraph, paragraph before
.TP
ESC Q K, ESC Q L
start of the line; the last character on the screen
.TP
ESC Q U, ESC Q V
end of the last word, of the next word
.TP
ESC Q H, ESC Q I
hyphenate; show the hyphenation
.TP
ESC (, ESC ) x L|B|S
mark; cut to buffer x (main, T-Z, or keystore 0-9): leave, blank,
squeeze (both ends in column 1: whole lines)
.TP
ESC . x L|B|S
the same for sentences
.TP
ESC * x E|I|O|D
paste: elbow, insert, overlay; empty the buffer
.TP
ESC } E|I|O
paste blanks
.TP
ESC - D, ESC - W, ESC - X
the last character, word, line taken out comes back
.TP
ESC F, ESC G, ESC - G
find, get (search forward), search back: the string ended by Return
ignores case and blanks, by Line feed it is exact; a/b, a&b and a\en
search for either, both on a screen, a in column n
.TP
ESC X n|0|W|U
repeat what follows n times, to the end, while, until
.TP
ESC =
repeat the last escape sequence
.TP
ESC A n, ESC B n
attribute n (1-5) on, off; ESC B L, ESC B W to the end of the line,
the next word; ESC B T, ESC B U show them
.TP
ESC B A|D, ESC B H|V A|D
sort lines; sort columns
.TP
ESC K, ESC T, ESC Y, ESC Q N
the date as yymmdd, short, in full; the time
.TP
ESC +
the calculator (Appendix D of LEX's manual: + - * / % = ( ) Q, P n,
K S x, K U, C, Z, F, I, N, S A R E x memories 0-9 A-Z, H V totals)
.TP
ESC S n, ESC n
give keystore n a text (to Return); type keystore n
.TP
ESC R n, ESC R S, ESC R C
the last keystrokes into keystore n; typed again; forgotten
.TP
ESC P
page break where the printing details put it
.TP
ESC L, ESC H
the status line; the last error explained
.TP
ESC S L, ESC S W, ESC S E
status line, page warning, errors shown (ESC - S ... off)
.TP
ESC M O|M|C|I|E|S|V|A|J|U
merge a document: open, all, close, a line, to the end, selected
lines, view, advance, skip, until
.TP
ESC W O|C|E|X|J|U
extract to a document
.TP
ESC O O|F|X
open a document from the playground
.TP
ESC C A|B|F|P
clear to the end; the background, the foreground of the screen; the
protected area
.TP
ESC C D
other document details (type)
.TP
ESC R D|N|M|O|V|W|I|G
type the document name, its key, the VMF name, the menu option, the
terminal record, the work file; show the system records, the merge file
.TP
ESC Q S, ESC Q T, ESC Q W
run a program: named in a record; asked for, waiting; asked for
.SH "RECORDS AND PROGRAMS"
In a program (a record performed, a keystore) the characters of
.B **MONT
stand for keys; in the Swedish one ( and : are the start and end
keys < and >, $ ESC, ' Ctrl, ; Return, @ the next character as it is,
"..." a comment, ! or ? a pause until Return.
Typing <KEY> performs the record KEY (<KEY+n/m> types characters n
to n+m-1 of it).
.TP 18
ESC > R|C|U|P|A|K
read, create, update, put (create or update), add to, kill the record
whose key follows the start key on the line (ESC ] ... with the
implicit returns)
.TP
ESC N, ESC - N, ESC Q Y
the next, previous key; the next deleted one
.TP
ESC S P, ESC U P
protect mode: only the fields (paint characters) can be typed in;
a record read or written is the fields in order, up to the cursor
.TP
ESC S B, ESC U B
background mode (typed text is protected)
.TP
ESC S H, ESC S U
shush, shut-up mode (ESC - S H, ESC - S U off)
.TP
ESC S M, ESC U M
mask on, off;
ESC S F, ESC U F a foreign system file;
ESC S Q x the squash character
.TP
ESC C T x
conditional: S string, K key, D document, F foreign key, N number at
the cursor, Q question, + - 0 accumulator; then two programs ended by
Return
.TP
ESC R K
perform a record named at a prompt
.SH "LIST PROCESSING"
An output format (a record) names a dictionary, P or V and the lines
per page, the record type, and options (/A /D sort, /T totals, /H help,
/P paint, /S spacing, /C print characters, /F foreign file, /U mark);
then headings, a blank line and the detail lines, where fields are
written with their formatting characters or as (NAME: in Swedish.
At the select prompt: NAME=value, >, <, : (contains), a-b, OCH, ELLER,
INTE, HELP; /K/K deletes what is selected.
Mailshot puts the fields of each record into a document.
.SH PRINTING
.B **PNST
asks which printer; each has a type (*$ escape sequences, *% for
combinations of attributes), a code table (*#, *( functions), and a
device or command.
In the document: .C print characters, .T titles, .F footers (\c
.B (SIDA:
or, in English,
.B (PAGE:
is the page number), .N footnotes, .P .PP page breaks, .S spacing,
.G keep lines together, .L to a line, .H halt, .I information, .M
another document, .X a program.
.PP
A printer of the type
.BR PDF ,
.B PDFSANS
or
.B PDFMONO
(printer 7 in the system files that come with
.IR nylex ;
it asks for the file's name) makes a PDF document instead, in Times,
Helvetica or Courier; one of the type
.BR PS ,
.B PSSANS
or
.B PSMONO
(printer 9, through lp) the same in PostScript, for a PostScript
printer or a file.
Courier keeps LEX's 10 characters to the inch.
In Times and Helvetica the text is set proportionally, as a daisy-wheel
printer with a proportional wheel set it: the lines of a justified
paragraph (J ruler) are spread to the margin, the other text starts at
its columns (12 to the inch, which these fonts about fill), numbers end
at theirs, and lines centred or against M stay so.
Attributes 1 and 4 underline, 2 and 5 are bold, 3 italic; footnote
numbers are raised.
The page is A4 (US Letter with
.BR LEXPAPER=letter ),
6 lines to the inch.
.PP
A daisy-wheel printer with a proportional wheel prints the same way when
its code table (*#) names a proportional spacing record
.BI ; NAME
(LEX-68's kind): for each character 32-126 the increments of 1/120 inch
to move before and after printing it, and at character 191 the wheel's
own petal table.
The printer is driven with Diablo 630's motion index (ESC US n), which
most daisy-wheel printers understand.
Printer 8 in the system files that come with
.I nylex
is such a printer (\c
.BR *$DIABLO ,
the made-up wheel
.BR ;PS10 );
the form
.B *PSFORM
(in the forms menu) shows and changes a wheel's record.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-f " file" ", /F" file
the system file (also
.BR $LEXVMF ).
.TP
.BI \-u " name" ", /U" name
the terminal record
.BI ) name
(also
.BR $LEXVDU ;
VT100 terminals need none).
.TP
.BI \-w " file" ", /W" file
the work file (also
.BR $LEXWORK ).
.TP
.BI // keys //
keys typed ahead, as a program.
.TP
.B \-c swe7\fR|\fBlatin1\fR|\fButf8
how the terminal shows the Swedish letters.
Documents keep them as LEX did, in 7-bit Swedish (ISO 646-SE:
[ \e ] { | } are the letters A-umlaut, O-umlaut, A-ring and their lower case).
With an English system file (its
.B **MONT
has English hyphenation) [ \e ] { | } are themselves, and the letters
of Latin-1 are kept as they are.
.B swe7
switches the terminal to 7-bit Swedish (on the D-NIX Amiga console),
.B latin1
(the default) and
.B utf8
(the default when the locale says UTF-8) translate.
.TP
.B \-V
the version.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
LEXVMF, LEXVDU, LEXWORK as above; LEXPRINT, a command that takes all
printing (instead of the printer of
.BR **PNST );
LEXPAPER (\c
.B a4
or
.BR letter )
and LEXPDF (the column and the size of the font of a PDF, in points:
.BR 7.2,15 )
for PDF printers.
.SH FILES
.TP
$HOME/nylex.vmf, $HOME/nylex.wrk
the system file, the work file
.TP
/usr/local/share/nylex/nylex.vmf, nylex-en.vmf
the system files that come with
.IR nylex ,
in Swedish and in English (on the D-NIX Amiga port in
/usr/modern/lib/nylex, and the source is in /usr/modern/src/nylex)
.TP
/usr/local/share/nylex/*.dict
the dictionaries of
.IR lexspell (1)
.TP
/usr/local/share/doc/nylex/TUTORIAL.md
a first tour of nylex, step by step
.SH SEE ALSO
lexvmf(1), lexspell(1), lexconv(1).
On the D-NIX Amiga port, lex68 runs LEX-68 itself as a demonstration
version.
.SH NOTES
The system files that come with
.I nylex
are its own, in Swedish and in English: menus, help pages, forms for the
installation, the printers, the document types and the printer codes,
the document index, an address register with lists, labels and
circular letters, an export and import (X in the document menu) that
runs
.IR lexconv (1)
for RTF, Word, OpenDocument, Markdown, HTML and text, and a spelling
menu that runs
.IR lexspell (1)
with a Swedish or a British English dictionary (the records
.BR *=SPLDEF x;
put en-US at character 33 for American English).
The English one uses the keys of the manual's German/Scandinavian table
(< and > start and end a key; ( : ; in programs) rather than those of
its English example ([ ] and \e), so that brackets can be typed; an
English
.B **MONT
that starts 091 works as the manual says.
With LEX-68's
.B lex9a1.vmf
its own menus, forms and programs run instead.
